  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1138 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nThe primary short-term forecast concern centers on fog potential\novernight. While satellite imagery depicts an eroding mid-level\ncloud deck (~6,000 ft), current dew point depressions of 3-\n6 degrees suggest a narrowing window for saturation. Confidence\nin widespread fog remains low due to a weak but persistent west-\nto-east pressure gradient, likely maintaining light boundary\nlayer mixing across the Mississippi River Valley. Consequently,\nexpect fog development to remain confined to sheltered valleys\nwhere decoupling is most probable.\n\nA highly amplified trough, especially for August standards, is\noriented from the eastern Great Lakes, southwest into the western\nGulf. This feature has been responsible for the mild\ntemperatures, cloudy skies and light rain we experienced over the\nweekend.  Sunday temperatures were 10 to 15 degrees below normal\nacross much of the Mid-South.  A strong ridge will build over the\nPlains today, shunting the trough east, becoming positioned over\nthe Appalachians this afternoon. As the ridge builds in from the\nwest, temperatures will trend closer to normal today. Expect\nhighs in the middle to upper 80s.\n\nBy midweek, the ridge will deamplify and transition across the\nMississippi River Valley, initiating a warming trend with low to\nmid-90s expected from Thursday through the weekend. This pattern\nwill suppress convection through Thursday, with only marginal\nrain chances (20-30%) returning late in the period.",
